# `bits` goes one direction only: it reinterprets a SIGNED integer or a float
# as the same-width unsigned integer. On an unsigned type it would be the
# identity, so it is not declared there and `u64.bits` is a missing member --
# not a conversion that quietly answers with the receiver. With one direction
# the name carries no ambiguity: `i64.bits` can only mean u64.
main: function is {
    a: 12.u64
    print "\{a.bits}"
}
